wineoss: the format AAudio granted is not the format we asked for, and nobody checked.

`finish_aaudio_stream` validates two of the three things that make a stream
interpretable and skips the third:

    if(AAudioStream_getSampleRate(aas) != fmt->nSamplesPerSec ||
            AAudioStream_getChannelCount(aas) != fmt->nChannels){

Rate and channels, never format. `AAudioStreamBuilder_setFormat` is documented
as a *request*: AAudio is free to open the stream in another format, and
`AAudioStream_getFormat` on the opened stream is the only way to learn what was
granted. The whole driver already knows this about the other two fields -- the
comment on the sharing-mode fallback says the mode granted is "logged rather than
believed" -- and then believes the format.

**What that costs is not a warning, it is noise.** `convert_to_i16` is decided
before the stream exists, on the guest's format alone
(`open_aaudio_stream`): the float branch sets it after asking for an exclusive
int16 stream, and the fallback path leaves it FALSE because it asked for float.
If that fallback open returns an int16 stream instead -- the ordinary outcome
when the HAL's only stereo/48k profile is `AUDIO_FORMAT_PCM_16_BIT`, which is
exactly what this device's `audio_policy_configuration.xml` says -- then the
driver writes IEEE-754 float bytes into a stream that reads them as PCM_16.

Sign bit, exponent and mantissa are then interpreted as two signed samples per
float. Ordinary audio lives in the -1.0..1.0 range, whose exponent bits are
mostly 0x3F80-ish patterns flipping constantly, so the result is not distorted
audio: it is broadband noise at the right level and the right rate, with the
stride wrong by a factor of two on top. **That is a complete buzz with the music
still nominally playing underneath**, and every counter in the driver reads
perfect while it happens -- which is what made this so hard to see. Requiem
buzzes end to end; Metro, whose stream is int16 and never reaches this branch,
does not.

FIVE THEORIES DIED BEFORE THIS ONE, and they are listed so none is retried:
breadcrumb instrumentation starving the audio thread; the 24 fps limit starving
the buffer; partial writes dropping samples; relaxed memory ordering corrupting
Wwise's mixing; and the two Wwise DSP plugins (`AkConvolutionReverb`,
`AkReflect`) being miscompiled under FEX. The first four were measured dead. The
fifth is the one this replaces: it was reached by elimination, and it was the
wrong answer to the right question -- *what does Requiem's audio do that Metro's
does not* -- because the answer turned out to be one branch in this file rather
than anything in the guest.

**The measurement that pointed here, and the reading error that hid it.** The
`aaudio:` line reports `format 1` = `AAUDIO_FORMAT_PCM_I16`, and that was read as
"Requiem asks for int16 and gets it", which retired the format question on
2026-08-16. It does not say that. `format` there is what AAudio granted, and it
is `I16` on *both* paths -- the converted-float one and the native-int16 one.
The line that distinguishes them is a `TRACE` on a channel Vessel's default
`WINEDEBUG` does not print. So the one fact that separated the two games was
observable, printed, and ambiguous.

So: reconcile after opening rather than before. The format the stream actually
has decides whether the conversion runs, which makes the two impossible to
disagree -- and a granted format that is neither the guest's nor int16-for-float
is refused with a line naming both, rather than played as noise.

`finish_aaudio_stream` takes the flag because it is the function that already
owns "did we get what we asked for", and both callers route through it. The
float branch's unconditional `*convert_to_i16 = TRUE` goes: it was correct only
by assuming its own request was honoured, which is the assumption this patch
exists to delete.

diff --git a/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c b/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c
--- a/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c
+++ b/dlls/wineoss.drv/oss.c
@@ -273,11 +273,11 @@
     TRACE("burst %d, capacity %d, client buffer %u, asked for %u, buffer size set to %d\n",
           burst, capacity, stream->bufsize_frames, want,
           AAudioStream_getBufferSizeInFrames(stream->aas));
 }
 
-static HRESULT finish_aaudio_stream(AAudioStream *aas, const WAVEFORMATEX *fmt)
+static HRESULT finish_aaudio_stream(AAudioStream *aas, const WAVEFORMATEX *fmt, BOOL *convert_to_i16)
 {
     /* The one line that says whether the latency below is going to be small.
      * ERR so it survives a session log at its default level: a burst this
      * driver did not expect is the first thing to look at when audio is rough,
      * and it costs one line per stream. */
@@ -291,12 +291,53 @@
     if(AAudioStream_getSampleRate(aas) != (int32_t)fmt->nSamplesPerSec ||
             AAudioStream_getChannelCount(aas) != fmt->nChannels){
         WARN("stream opened at %d Hz x%d, wanted %u Hz x%u\n",
                 AAudioStream_getSampleRate(aas), AAudioStream_getChannelCount(aas),
                 (unsigned)fmt->nSamplesPerSec, fmt->nChannels);
         AAudioStream_close(aas);
         return AUDCLNT_E_UNSUPPORTED_FORMAT;
     }
 
+    /* **The format is decided here because here is where it is known.**
+     *
+     * The two fields above are checked against what was asked for; the format
+     * was not checked at all, and `convert_to_i16` was decided by the caller
+     * before this stream existed. `AAudioStreamBuilder_setFormat` is a request
+     * -- AAudio may open the stream in another format and say so only through
+     * `AAudioStream_getFormat` -- so a float guest whose fallback open quietly
+     * returned int16 had float bytes written into an int16 stream. That is not
+     * a distortion, it is broadband noise: the exponent bits of ordinary
+     * -1.0..1.0 samples read as PCM are pure hash, at the right level and the
+     * right rate, which is a buzz over audio that is otherwise still there.
+     *
+     * Deciding it from the granted format instead makes the flag and the stream
+     * impossible to disagree, which is the only property that matters -- the
+     * old code was correct exactly when its request happened to be honoured. */
+    {
+        aaudio_format_t granted = AAudioStream_getFormat(aas);
+        aaudio_format_t wanted = get_aaudio_format(fmt);
+
+        if(granted == wanted){
+            *convert_to_i16 = FALSE;
+        }else if(granted == AAUDIO_FORMAT_PCM_I16 && wanted == AAUDIO_FORMAT_PCM_FLOAT){
+            /* The pairing this driver knows how to bridge, and the one it goes
+             * looking for: MMAP NOIRQ takes int16 and nothing else on this
+             * device, so an int16 stream for a float guest is the good outcome
+             * rather than a fallback. It just has to be observed rather than
+             * assumed. */
+            MESSAGE("aaudio: int16 stream for a float guest; converting on the way out\n");
+            *convert_to_i16 = TRUE;
+        }else{
+            /* Neither the guest's format nor a pair we can bridge. Refusing is
+             * the only honest option: writing the guest's bytes into it would
+             * produce noise, and mmdevapi can still fall back to a format this
+             * device does take. */
+            MESSAGE("aaudio: stream opened as format %d, wanted %d, and there is no "
+                    "conversion between them\n", granted, wanted);
+            AAudioStream_close(aas);
+            return AUDCLNT_E_UNSUPPORTED_FORMAT;
+        }
+    }
+
     return S_OK;
 }
 
@@ -348,13 +389,12 @@
         if(AAudioStreamBuilder_openStream(builder, out) == AAUDIO_OK){
             HRESULT hr;
 
             TRACE("exclusive int16 stream for a float guest; converting on the way out\n");
             AAudioStreamBuilder_delete(builder);
-            if(FAILED(hr = finish_aaudio_stream(*out, fmt))){
+            if(FAILED(hr = finish_aaudio_stream(*out, fmt, convert_to_i16))){
                 *out = NULL;
                 return hr;
             }
-            *convert_to_i16 = TRUE;
             return S_OK;
         }
         TRACE("no exclusive int16 stream; keeping the guest's own format\n");
@@ -401,7 +441,7 @@
     {
-        HRESULT hr = finish_aaudio_stream(*out, fmt);
+        HRESULT hr = finish_aaudio_stream(*out, fmt, convert_to_i16);
 
         if(FAILED(hr)) *out = NULL;
         return hr;
     }
 }
